tell the game about the shell the first time it is told

`set_sp_frame` assigned the game's `shell` flag after its early return, and
the two fields it keeps in step started life disagreeing: the compositor's
`bezel_shown` is true at construction, the game's `shell` is false. So the
first call of `set_sp_frame(true)` was "no change" and returned before the
game ever heard about it.

What that looked like on the handheld: a cart booted into directly -- powered
off in a game and switched back on, which is slot's normal resume -- drew its
picture centred on the panel instead of at the top under the Advance SP
shell's opening, so the shell's chin covered the last rows of the game.
Ejecting to the shelf and going back in fixed it, because then the flag
genuinely moved. Reported from a Mini Plus as "the bottom of the GBA game
looks cut off, like the bezel is too big", and "I'll exit and re-enter the
game and it will be different" -- which is the whole bug in one sentence.

The flag is assigned before the early return now, so the two cannot drift
apart again whatever either side was initialised to.

The test boots a compositor, says `set_sp_frame(true)` once, and asks where
the picture sits; against the old code it is 26 rows down, which is exactly
where the chin starts eating it.

This is the second bug of the same shape in a week: a fast path that skips
work when a flag already holds the value being set, where the flag's starting
value made the first real change invisible. `0024` was the other.

Applies to upstream 91499a9.

